## Summary

`frontend/src/_ui/Avatar/index.jsx` never revokes the blob URLs it creates, because the cleanup function is written but never returned. Separately, the same effect has no guard against a stale response, so a row in the virtualized user list can end up showing a different user's picture.

## The code

```js
React.useEffect(() => {
    async function fetchAvatar() {
      const blob = await userService.getAvatar(avatarId);
      setAvatar(URL.createObjectURL(blob));
    }
    if (avatarId) fetchAvatar();

    () => avatar && URL.revokeObjectURL(avatar);
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
  }, [avatarId]);
```

Line 18 is missing a `return`. As written it constructs an arrow function and discards it, so `URL.revokeObjectURL` is never reached. The intent is clearly there, it just is not wired up. Every other place in the frontend that creates a blob URL does revoke it properly, `Camera.jsx`, `PDF.jsx`, `Chat/index.js`, `_lib/generate-file.js` and `BulkUploadDrawer`, which is what made this one stand out.

`no-unused-expressions` is not enabled in `eslint.config.mjs`, so nothing flagged it.

## Impact

`Avatar` is rendered per row in `UsersTable.jsx` and in `VirtualizedUserList.jsx`. Because the list is virtualized, `avatarId` changes on the same mounted component as rows are recycled during scrolling, so this is not a one-off allocation. Each change fetches a new blob and leaks the previous URL for the lifetime of the page.

The same recycling causes the second problem. There is no ignore flag, so if a request for the previous `avatarId` resolves after the component has moved to a new one, the late response calls `setAvatar` and overwrites the correct picture. In a members list that means a row showing the wrong person's avatar, which is more confusing than a blank one.

## Expected vs actual

Expected: the object URL for an avatar is released when the avatar changes or the component unmounts, and a response that arrives after `avatarId` has moved on is discarded.

Actual: no URL is ever released, and a late response wins.

## Notes

I have a fix ready with tests, three cases covering revoke-on-unmount, revoke-on-id-change and ignoring the stale response. All three fail against `main` and pass with the change. I am happy to open the PR, or to leave it if you would rather assign this elsewhere. Just say which.

One thing I ran into while writing the tests, which is separate from this issue and I am not proposing to fix here: the frontend jest config sets `"testEnvironment": "jest-environment-jsdom"` but `jest-environment-jsdom` is not in `frontend/package.json`. Since Jest 28 it is not bundled, so on a fresh clone `npx jest` fails with `Test environment jest-environment-jsdom cannot be found` before running anything. Existing tests such as `src/_helpers/__tests__/validateName.test.js` hit the same wall. No CI workflow runs frontend jest so nothing is red because of it, but it does mean the suite cannot be run locally without installing that package by hand. Happy to file it separately if useful.

﻿## Bug Description

App.jsx starts a setInterval for metadata refresh but never stores the return value and never clears it. If the component is unmounted and remounted, a new interval is added each time without cancelling the previous one. Stale intervals continue making authenticated HTTP requests after the user logs out.

## Affected file

 rontend/src/App/App.jsx, line 133:

`js
setInterval(this.fetchMetadata, 1000 * 60 * 60 * 1);
// return value discarded - interval can never be cleared
`

For contrast, ViewerApp.jsx line 112 correctly stores and clears the interval:
`js
// ViewerApp.jsx - correct pattern
this._metadataInterval = setInterval(this.fetchMetadata, interval);
// componentWillUnmount:
clearInterval(this._metadataInterval);
`

## Failure scenario

1. User logs in. App mounts. Interval 1 starts, making metadata requests every hour.
2. HMR reload in development (or future routing change) unmounts and remounts App. Interval 2 starts.
3. Interval 1 is still running - now two concurrent hourly requests fire.
4. Over repeated HMR cycles, N intervals accumulate.
5. User logs out. Stale intervals continue calling  etchMetadata, triggering 401 responses and unnecessary network activity for the session lifetime.

## Fix

`js
componentDidMount() {
  this._metadataInterval = setInterval(this.fetchMetadata, 1000 * 60 * 60 * 1);
  // ... rest of componentDidMount
}

componentWillUnmount() {
  clearInterval(this._metadataInterval);
}
`

## Environment

ToolJet main branch (2026-08-13), React (class component).

﻿## Bug Description

Every ToolJet DB management endpoint accepts organizationId as a URL path parameter and passes it directly to the service. The ability guard checks whether the caller is authenticated (and for writes, whether they are an admin) but never verifies that the URL's organizationId matches the caller's own organization. Any authenticated user can enumerate the table schemas of any organization, and any organization admin can drop or mutate tables in any other organization.

## Affected files

**server/src/modules/tooljet-db/ability/index.ts, line 78** - VIEW grants have no organization boundary:
`	s
can([FEATURE_KEY.VIEW_TABLE, FEATURE_KEY.VIEW_TABLES, FEATURE_KEY.JOIN_TABLES], InternalTable);
`

**server/src/modules/tooljet-db/controller.ts, lines 69-245** - all endpoints pass URL param to service:
`	s
// GET /tooljet-db/organizations/:organizationId/tables
@Get(':organizationId/tables')
async tables(@Param('organizationId') organizationId: string, ...) {
  return await this.tooljetDbService.allTables(organizationId, ...);
}
`

Write ability check reads from the request header (caller's own org), not the URL:
`	s
if (superAdmin || isAdmin || userPermission.tjdbCRUD) {
  can([FEATURE_KEY.CREATE_TABLE, FEATURE_KEY.DROP_TABLE, FEATURE_KEY.ADD_COLUMN, ...], InternalTable);
}
`

## Failure scenarios

**Read (any authenticated user):** Caller logs into their own org, sends GET /api/tooljet-db/organizations/<victim-org-uuid>/tables with their own valid JWT. The CASL VIEW check passes. Response returns the full table schema catalog of the victim org.

**Write (org admin):** An admin of org A sends DELETE /api/tooljet-db/organizations/<org-B-uuid>/table/users. The admin ability check passes (admin of their own org). The controller passes org B's UUID to dropTable, which issues DROP TABLE against org B's Postgres schema.

## Fix

In FeatureAbilityFactory.defineAbilityFor or in a new request guard, assert that params.organizationId === user.organizationId. The safest fix is to derive organizationId exclusively from the authenticated user's session and ignore the URL parameter entirely:

`	s
// In service layer, always use:
const organizationId = user.organizationId; // from JWT
// Never: const organizationId = params.organizationId
`

## Environment

ToolJet main branch (2026-08-13), NestJS, TypeORM.
